# Never manage secrets or credential material with chezmoi.
# Anything listed here is ignored on `apply` and must never be `add`ed.

.git-credentials
.netrc
.ssh/**
.aws/**
.gnupg/**
.config/gcloud/**
.config/github-copilot/**
.docker/config.json
.npmrc
.pypirc
.gradle/gradle.properties
.m2/settings.xml

# Machine-local zsh modules. Employer-specific and credential-adjacent config
# lives here (VPN helpers, work-only tooling) and must not follow the dotfiles
# to another machine. ~/.zshrc sources this directory if it exists.
.config/zsh/local/**

# ~/.gitconfig is the identity half of git's user-level config: `[user]`,
# employer host rewrites, corporate hook templateDirs, per-machine overrides.
# This repo configures tooling, not people, so it does not manage the file at
# all -- not even as a template with the identity templated out. What it does
# manage is ~/.config/git/config, git's second user-level file, which holds the
# pager, the editor and the delta theme. git reads both.
.gitconfig

# Machine-local state, caches, and build artifacts
.local/state/**
.cache/**
.zsh_history
.zcompdump*
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# There was a nine-entry block here for "stale backups that predate version
# control" -- .zshrc.bak-*, .config/nvim.bak/**, .config/spaceship.zsh and the
# rest. Every one of those paths has since been deleted, and they were one-time
# migration artifacts that cannot come back, so the patterns were guarding
# nothing. Entries below are kept even when the path is currently absent, because
# those are files a tool could recreate.

# zsh plugins are git clones, managed by their own upstreams
.config/zsh/plugins/**

# tmux plugins are tpm-managed git clones
.config/tmux/plugins/**

# Karabiner is machine-local, for two independent reasons: its remaps are keyed
# to one external keyboard's vendor_id/product_id, and the profile carries a
# work VPN hotkey -- employer-specific, so it must not follow this repo to a
# personal laptop. Karabiner also rewrites and reindents the file on every
# settings change, which showed up as permanent `chezmoi diff` noise; worse, an
# `apply` from the stored copy silently deleted the VPN rule, because the app
# had added it after the file was last captured. Templating is not an option
# here -- `chezmoi re-add` skips templates, and this file is app-owned.
.config/karabiner/**

# VS Code, for the same app-owned reason as Karabiner plus one of its own. This
# setup is terminal-first -- the managed IDE is Neovim, and GUI editor settings
# are outside this repository -- so a VS Code config has nothing to say here.
#
# Its settings.json used to be managed, and it was never really authored: it was
# captured off one Mac wholesale, so whatever the installed extensions had
# written became committed configuration. That is how a corporate scanner's CLI
# path, a GitLab Duo language list and a vendor telemetry opt-in ended up in this
# repo. Removing them was not enough either -- the extensions wrote all four
# straight back into the live file, so the next `apply` refused to overwrite and
# needed `--force`. The file is owned by the app, not by us.
Library/Application Support/Code/**
# ~/.vscode too -- it is the extensions directory, but it also held a
# settings.json pinning an FVM SDK under an absolute home path. Guarded rather
# than dropped when the directory was deleted, because reinstalling the editor
# recreates it: the sibling entry above was deleted this morning and was back by
# 09:37, regenerated with an internal GitLab URL in it.
.vscode/**

# Cursor, for the same reason and then some. The GUI app is neither installed
# nor configured by this repository; keep the guards because a manual install
# can still create sensitive machine-local state.
#
# These entries exist for `add`, not for `apply`: nothing is managed at these
# paths, so there is nothing to skip on apply. What they refuse is the accidental
# capture. Checked, rather than assumed -- `chezmoi add --dry-run ~/.cursor/mcp.json`
# reported no warning and would have captured an internal GitLab hostname, the
# same string that cost two history rewrites to remove. The VS Code entry above
# already refused the equivalent add; these did not exist yet.
#
# What lives there today: an MCP server pointing at an employer GitLab instance,
# a dart server whose command is an absolute path containing a username and a
# pinned FVM version, and a settings.json carrying a corporate scanner's CLI
# path, an AI provider and a set of terminal auto-approve decisions. Correct for
# one machine, wrong for every other.
.cursor/**
Library/Application Support/Cursor/**

# aider's own state, denied with holes for its three managed configs.
#
# aider writes .aider.input.history, .aider.chat.history.md and a
# .aider.tags.cache.v* symbol cache into whatever directory it runs in. In a
# project it offers to gitignore them itself; in $HOME nothing would, and a
# chat transcript is the single most sensitive file this setup can produce --
# it contains whatever source was in context. The config, model settings and
# model metadata files below carry no credentials. aider uses local Ollama and
# has no API key at all.
.aider*
!.aider.conf.yml
!.aider.model.settings.yml
!.aider.model.metadata.json

# ~/.claude, denied as a tree with one hole in it.
#
# This repo manages exactly one thing under there -- skills/c4-architect, the C4
# modelling prompt -- and that single managed path is the whole reason the rest
# needs an explicit guard. Before it existed, nothing here touched ~/.claude and
# there was no reason to `add` anything from it.
#
# What the rest of the tree holds: projects/*/memory/*.md, which is a written
# record of who the machine's owner is -- role, employer, the tooling they were
# told about -- plus full session transcripts, pasted file contents and
# credentials-adjacent debugging output. None of that is configuration and all of
# it is machine-local.
#
# The stakes are higher than the Cursor entries above. This repo now has a public
# remote, so an accidental capture here is not a diff to notice and revert --
# it is published, cached by third parties, and permanent even after a
# force-push. Deny the tree, allow only skills.
# Note the middle line. `**` matches ZERO or more path segments, so `.claude/**`
# matches `.claude` itself -- which prunes the whole directory and takes the
# skills exception below it with it, silently. Checked: without `!.claude`,
# `chezmoi ignored` lists `.claude` and `chezmoi managed` never mentions the
# skill. Later patterns win, so the order here matters too.
.claude/**
!.claude
!.claude/skills/**

# ~/.claude.json is a SIBLING of ~/.claude/, so the rule above does not touch it.
# `.claude/**` matches paths INSIDE that directory; this is a file next to it,
# and the two are unrelated as far as globbing is concerned.
#
# That mattered more than it sounds: checked with `chezmoi add --dry-run`, and it
# offered to create private_dot_claude.json rather than refusing. The file is
# 72 KB of session state -- absolute project paths, usage counters, an
# oauthAccount key. README.md has claimed all along that this file "is not
# managed by chezmoi, on purpose", which was true only because nobody had run
# `add` on it. Documented protection that nothing enforced.
.claude.json
.claude.json.backup

# Credential and identity caches belonging to tools this repo does not manage but
# that are installed on the machine anyway.
#
# configstore is where firebase-tools keeps its OAuth token cache; flutter's
# settings file records the Apple Developer signing identity, which is an email
# address. Both regenerate per machine, so there is nothing to carry even if they
# were safe -- and they are not.
.config/configstore/**
.config/flutter/settings

# Repo documentation and tooling. Without these, chezmoi treats a source-root
# file as a target and writes it into $HOME -- CHEATSHEET.md showed up as a
# pending ~/CHEATSHEET.md until it was listed here, and bootstrap.sh as a
# pending ~/bootstrap.sh.
README.md
INSTALL.md
CLAUDE.md
CHEATSHEET.md
bootstrap.sh
# The previous two-line powerline starship config. Kept as a file rather than
# left in git history, because the history gets squashed and rewritten -- a
# comment pointing at a commit is a promise this repo cannot keep.
starship-powerline.toml
